<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  The four entitlements the jailbroken variant needs, and nothing else.

  Measured as a matrix of seven variants on iOS 26.6.2; two of the results are
  counter-intuitive and are the reason this file is exactly this short:

    - The sandbox escape is the gate for BOTH capabilities. A containerized
      process gets EPERM from proc_listallpids no matter which task-port
      entitlements it carries — adding every one of these to a containerized
      app changed nothing at all.
    - Injection then needs EXACTLY task_for_pid-allow. Apple's own
      com.apple.system-task-ports does not work, and neither does
      platform-application. The kernel's task_for_pid check reads the old one.

  So: no-sandbox + no-container make listing possible, task_for_pid-allow makes
  injecting possible on top of that.

  The runningboard entitlement then decides whether a process may *run*, which
  is a separate axis from whether we may inject into it. iOS suspends an app
  within about a second of it leaving the foreground, and a suspended process
  has no thread scheduled to run injected code in — so without it, injecting
  into an app always times out and this variant stops answering as soon as it
  is not on screen. Daemons are unaffected either way; the system does not
  suspend them, which is why injection worked for them first.

  process-state does two jobs, and the second is the load-bearing one:

    - It lets us ask whether a *different* process is actually running, so a
      target that is still suspended fails immediately instead of waiting out
      the injector's 20-second verdict budget and reporting a bare timeout.
      (Asking about this process needs no entitlement.)
    - It is what lets the suspension assertion target another process at all.
      The legacy attribute's originator check wants a platform binary or any
      entitlement in domain 63, and this is in that domain. Without it, this
      variant could keep only itself awake — which would leave injecting into
      an app exactly as impossible as it was before.

  What is NOT here, and why it cannot be:
  com.apple.runningboard.primitiveattribute would be the obvious way to do all
  this, and it does not work. It is a *restricted* entitlement: runningboardd
  strips it from any process whose bundle identifier is not in the allow-list
  at /System/Library/RunningBoard/runningboardEntitlementsConfiguration.plist
  — read off the device, about fifteen Apple identities, with no way for a
  third party to join them. Signing it in achieves nothing and was measured
  doing nothing. The assertion uses RBSLegacyAttribute instead, which is
  validated by a path that never consults that list.

  com.apple.runningboard.launchprocess is absent for an ordinary scope reason:
  it would allow launching an app that has never run, which is a separate
  proposal and a wider grant than this needs.

  Measurements and addresses:
  Documentations/Evolutions/draft-device-process-assertions.md.

  Xcode cannot sign these — no provisioning profile grants them — so a build
  installed by Xcode cannot inject. The variant is signed ad-hoc with these
  entitlements and installed by something that honours them; vphoned preserves
  each binary's existing entitlements when it installs, and a jailbreak
  installer grants them outright.

  ENABLE_APP_SANDBOX is NO for this target on purpose: it would generate
  com.apple.security.app-sandbox, which directly contradicts no-sandbox.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.no-container</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.private.security.no-sandbox</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.runningboard.process-state</key>
	<true/>
	<key>task_for_pid-allow</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
